---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Cross-product SSO">
  <h1>Cross-product SSO</h1>
  <p>
    One authentication at the auth origin, many independently minted
    per-product sessions. A user who completes the
    <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}/docs/auth/oauth`}>hosted OAuth
    flow</a> at the auth origin additionally gets a server-side SSO session;
    every product in the same project can then sign them in with a single
    browser navigation — no second credential prompt — while still receiving
    its <strong>own</strong> fresh token pair.
  </p>

  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      The hosted OAuth callback mints the SSO session: an opaque token in a
      <code>__Host-sso_session</code> cookie (HttpOnly, Secure,
      <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, <code>Path=/</code>, no
      <code>Domain</code> — the browser pins it to the exact auth origin
      host). Only the token's SHA-256 hash is stored server-side, with its
      own TTL.
    </li>
    <li>
      A product links to
      <code>GET /sso/continue?return_to=&lt;app url&gt;</code> on the auth
      origin.
    </li>
    <li>
      Continue-as validates the session and <strong>re-runs every
      authorization gate</strong>: the return-URL allowlist, project access
      mode, account status, the tenant login policy with the
      <em>original</em> login method (a second factor now required falls
      back to a full login), and the product age gate.
    </li>
    <li>
      The user lands back at <code>return_to?code=&lt;otc&gt;</code> and the
      product redeems the single-use code exactly as after a hosted login
      (<code>RedeemOAuthCode</code>) — inheriting refresh rotation and
      replay detection. Token pairs are never shared across products, and
      sessions never bridge projects.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    With no valid session, <code>/sso/continue</code> redirects to the
    hosted sign-in page, preserving the destination. With
    <code>GATEWAY_SSO_CONTINUE_MODE=one_tap</code>, a confirmation page is
    shown first and the code is minted only on the confirming POST.
  </p>

  <h2>Enabling it</h2>
  <p>
    Off by default — a disabled deployment registers no
    <code>/sso</code> routes and mints no session.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`# Required. Enabling without GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS
# (the allowlist continue-as validates return_to against) fails at boot.
GATEWAY_SSO_ENABLED=true
GATEWAY_OAUTH_ALLOWED_RETURN_URLS=https://product-a.example.com/callback

# Absolute session lifetime, no sliding renewal (default 28800 = 8h).
GATEWAY_SSO_SESSION_TTL_SECONDS=28800

# silent (default) | one_tap.
GATEWAY_SSO_CONTINUE_MODE=silent`}
  />

  <h2>Sign-out semantics</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Per-product logout</strong> revokes only that product's
      tokens. The SSO session survives — signing out of one product is not
      signing out of the auth origin.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Sign-out-everywhere</strong> (<code>RevokeAllSessions</code>)
      revokes every session store, SSO included, and works for
      password-less (OAuth-only) accounts.
    </li>
    <li>
      The SSO session dies with the credential it was minted from: password
      reset, email change, planted-credential clearing, account deletion
      (including the sweeper purge), and refresh-replay detection all
      revoke it — a leftover cookie can never continue-as afterwards.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
